From: Warden Project <warden@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tlv320aic31xx: trace regulator probe-defer in dmesg

devm_regulator_bulk_get() returning -EPROBE_DEFER is the normal,
expected outcome when this codec's supply regulators are registered
by a driver that probes after this one in a custom BSP -- the
kernel will retry the probe later. The current code deliberately
stays silent in that case (only logging real failures), which is
correct for production dmesg noise, but makes it hard to tell, while
bringing up a new board, whether the driver is stuck in a defer loop
or simply never probed at all: both look identical in dmesg.

Add a dev_dbg() trace on the deferred path so `dyndbg` can be
switched on during bring-up to see the defer happening without
changing the normal (non-debug) log output at all.

Found while integrating this codec into a custom infotainment audio
stack where the board's own PMIC regulator driver initializes after
the audio codec in probe order, causing a real EPROBE_DEFER on every
cold boot until the PMIC driver finishes -- with no way to see that
in dmesg without this trace.

Signed-off-by: Warden Project <warden@example.invalid>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tlv320aic31xx.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tlv320aic31xx.c
index e0a8db4..1a2b3c4 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tlv320aic31xx.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tlv320aic31xx.c
@@ -1661,6 +1661,8 @@ static int aic31xx_i2c_probe(struct i2c_client *i2c,
 		if (ret != -EPROBE_DEFER)
 			dev_err(aic31xx->dev,
 				"Failed to request supplies: %d\n", ret);
+		else
+			dev_dbg(aic31xx->dev, "supply request deferred\n");
 		return ret;
 	}

